      "id": "bbc-2026-07-07-people-fixing-the-world",
      "title": "People Fixing The World",
      "summary": "The people turning life experience into business ideas with funding and other support",
      "body": "Home            News            Sport            Weather            iPlayer            Sounds            Bitesize     CBeebies     CBBC     Food         Home     News     Sport     Business     Technology     Health     Culture     Arts     Travel     Earth     Audio     Video     Live          Use BBC.com or the new BBC App to listen to BBC podcasts, Radio 4 and the World Service outside the UK.Find out how to listen to other BBC stationsEpisode detailsWorld Service,·07 Jul 2026,·23 minsAvailable for over a yearAt least once in a lifetime, everyone has a great business idea. They often come when life events force us to experience things from a different angle. This year, the city of Liverpool in north England set about finding these “hidden entrepreneurs” with the help of an organisation called Public Life. The entrepreneurs come from all walks of life and have been offered a year’s wage to develop their idea, alongside expert help and mentoring. From the hairdresser who is building a salon-on-wheels for her best friend to the travel agent who is making a \"Trip Advisor for the senses\", Myra Anubi meets the people whose inspiration has come from their own life experiences.\nPeople Fixing The World from the BBC is about brilliant solutions to the world's problems. We release a new edition every Tuesday. We'd love you to let us know what you think and to hear about your own solutions. You can contact us on WhatsApp by messaging +44 8000 321721 or email peoplefixingtheworld@bbc.co.uk. And please leave us a review on your chosen podcast provider.\n(Image: Myra Anubi with Frankii Panchoo in Liverpool, BBC)\nPublic Life website - www.publiclife.org.ukProgramme WebsiteMore episodes",
